-- title:
--   Inertia field of a place over ℚ(ζₚ) is unramified
-- statement:
--   Let $p$ be a prime, and let $L$ be a field of characteristic zero that is a cyclotomic extension of $\mathbb{Q}$ of order $\{p\}$, so $L=\mathbb{Q}(\zeta_p)$. Let $A$ be a commutative domain which is a discrete valuation ring, equipped with an algebra structure over which $L$ is its fraction field, and assume $p$ lies in the maximal ideal of $A$. Fix compatible embeddings of $A$ and of $L$ into $\mathrm{AlgebraicClosure}\ \mathbb{Q}$ forming a scalar tower, and let $Pl$ be a valuation subring of $\mathrm{AlgebraicClosure}\ \mathbb{Q}$ such that $(p)$ is a non-unit of $Pl$, and such that every element of $A$ maps into $Pl$. Write $H$ for the intersection of the image in $\mathrm{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}/\mathbb{Q})$ of the inertia subgroup of $Pl$ (transported along the inclusion of the decomposition subgroup) with the subgroup fixing the image of $L$ in $\mathrm{AlgebraicClosure}\ \mathbb{Q}$, let $T'$ be the fixed field of $H$, and let $O$ be the preimage of $Pl$ in $T'$ under $T' \to \mathrm{AlgebraicClosure}\ \mathbb{Q}$. The conclusion is threefold: $O$ is a discrete valuation ring; every element of the maximal ideal of $A$ maps to a non-unit of $Pl$; and for every $o$ in the maximal ideal of $O$ there are $a$ in the maximal ideal of $A$ and $o'\in O$ with $o = a\,o'$ as elements of $\mathrm{AlgebraicClosure}\ \mathbb{Q}$, i.e. $\mathfrak{m}_O = \mathfrak{m}_A O$.
--
--   This is the statement that the inertia field of a place of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ above $p$, taken relative to the base $L=\mathbb{Q}(\zeta_p)$, is unramified over the local ring $A$ at the place below: the valuation ring cut out on the inertia field is a discrete valuation ring whose maximal ideal is generated by that of $A$. It feeds the construction of a ring homomorphism out of $A$ into the valuation ring of the inertia field with prescribed behaviour on maximal ideals, used in the local analysis of the Galois representations attached to the Frey curve at $p$.
